# Mix Tape — licensing

Mix Tape is not licensed as one thing. The licence depends on which directory a file lives in, and the split is deliberate.

| Path | Licence | Full text |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Shared/` | MIT | [`Shared/LICENSE`](Shared/LICENSE) |
| `App/` | MIT | [`App/LICENSE`](App/LICENSE) |
| `Server/` | AGPL-3.0-only | [`Server/LICENSE`](Server/LICENSE) |

Everything outside those three directories — this file, `docs/`, `scripts/` — is MIT.

## Why the split points this way, and why it must not be "tidied"

**MIT flows into AGPL. AGPL does not flow into MIT.**

The app links `Shared`. If `Shared` were AGPL, the MIT claim on `App` would be invalid, because an AGPL dependency makes the whole combined work AGPL. So `Shared` must stay permissive. That is the load-bearing part of this arrangement, and it is easy to get backwards.

The server is AGPL because it is a self-hosted network service, which is exactly the case AGPL exists for: someone who runs a modified Mix Tape server for other people has to publish their changes.

Every source file carries an `SPDX-License-Identifier` line matching its directory, and [`scripts/check-spdx.sh`](scripts/check-spdx.sh) fails the build if one is missing or wrong. If you move a file between these directories, its SPDX line moves with it and must be corrected.

